<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conrad II of Znojmo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1161">1161</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conrad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> was a Přemyslid prince who ruled the Moravian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of Znojmo in the 12th century. He led the Moravian princes who challenged Duke Vladislaus II for the Bohemian throne, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1142. Defeated after Vladislaus obtained help from King Conrad III, he later reconciled with the duke.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-of-znojmo.jpg" alt="Conrad II of Znojmo" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous fresco in Znojmo (CZ) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konrad_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Conrad was the son of Prince Luitpold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> and his wife Ida, a daughter of the Babenberg margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. When his father died in 1112, Znojmo passed to his uncle Ulrich I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, who died the following year. From 1115 both the Brno and Znojmo principalities were held by Soběslav I, the youngest son of the late King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vratislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vratislaus II of Bohemia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Conrad first ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> from 1123, when Soběslav fell out with his elder brother, Duke Vladislaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Vladislaus died two years later, and as the dynastic struggle came to a head at the Battle of Chlumec in 1126, Soběslav had Conrad deposed in 1128 and imprisoned at Dohna Castle. Seeking to reconcile with his Moravian cousin and to steady his rule, Soběslav later restored him as prince of Znojmo, which he held again from 1134 until his death, and arranged his marriage to the Vukanović princess Maria, a daughter of Grand Prince Uroš I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>.</p><p>The quarrels revived when Vladislaus II, son of Vladislaus I, came to the Bohemian throne in 1140. Two years later Conrad and his cousins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, but the castle was held by the duke's younger brother, Prince Děpold I of Jamnitz, while Vladislaus went to the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and returned with a large royal army. The Moravian forces had to retire, and Conrad, excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jindrich-zdik">Jindřich Zdík</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Olomouc, had to accept Bohemian overlordship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Some time after 1134 Conrad commissioned the wall paintings of the Znojmo Rotunda. He died in 1161. He and Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> had five children. Their son Conrad III Ota married Hellicha of Wittelsbach, became the first Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in 1182 and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1189, and their daughter Helena of Znojmo became High Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> by her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just">Casimir II the Just</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conrad II of Znojmo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Conrad II of Znojmo</b> was a strong contender for the Bohemian throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-znojmo">Duchy of Znojmo</a></li></ul></section>
